WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.878 --> 00:00:04.129
What we're seeing today,

00:00:05.088 --> 00:00:08.300
is dramatic evidence of what
happens when you fail

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.010
to talk honestly about
your history.

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:14.264
And it harkens to the mid- 
19 century

00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:15.098
during the Civil War.

00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:19.061
So Lincoln becomes
important to reflect

00:00:19.353 --> 00:00:21.396
on the issues of division that
we're seeing today.

00:00:24.775 --> 00:00:26.360
Abraham Lincoln has outwitted

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.363
just about as many historians as
politicians of his own day.

00:00:29.696 --> 00:00:31.406
Unless you understand
what is going on

00:00:31.406 --> 00:00:32.866
around what he is saying.

00:00:33.242 --> 00:00:35.494
You can never really understand
Abraham Lincoln.

00:00:35.786 --> 00:00:38.664
♪♪

00:00:38.664 --> 00:00:41.458
Lincoln did not start
his presidency

00:00:41.458 --> 00:00:43.627
to be the great emancipator.

00:00:43.627 --> 00:00:46.380
It's not a phrase Lincoln asked
to be applied to himself

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:47.589
and we can do better.

00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:50.384
♪♪

00:00:50.384 --> 00:00:53.845
Frederick Douglass
is every bit the equal

00:00:53.845 --> 00:00:55.472
of the president of the
United States.

00:00:55.472 --> 00:00:57.683
He's constantly in Lincoln's ear

00:00:57.683 --> 00:00:59.601
pushing him on black humanity.

00:01:00.143 --> 00:01:02.521
As the voices that have
access to Lincoln

00:01:02.521 --> 00:01:04.564
change Lincoln himself

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:05.440
changes in response.

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:09.278
♪♪

00:01:12.406 --> 00:01:15.492
Lincoln's number one
agenda is unification.

00:01:15.993 --> 00:01:18.453
It is not the
abolition of slavery.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:20.497
♪♪

00:01:21.248 --> 00:01:24.126
Lincoln was forced to
reckon with slavery

00:01:24.126 --> 00:01:25.961
due to black people
themeselves.

00:01:26.628 --> 00:01:28.839
He came to think that
out of this would come

00:01:28.839 --> 00:01:30.590
something truly monumental.

00:01:32.009 --> 00:01:34.845
Lincoln is talking about doing
something pretty radical here.

00:01:34.845 --> 00:01:38.098
Something really important
inside of him was opening up.

00:01:38.307 --> 00:01:39.683
He was trying to navigate

00:01:39.683 --> 00:01:42.602
the currents of
really irreconciable ideas.

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:47.107
♪♪

